{#- Each highlight tab's one plain sentence and its "Exact rules" fold: one macro per `highlights.Tab.key`, called
    with `r`, the thresholds `html._rule_values` formats. Edit a list's wording here; its screen is in screens.py.
    The rules are short bullets, one theme each, jargon glossed on first use (user's choice, Nov 3, 2026). -#}
{% macro rules(summary) %}<p class="list-summary">{{ summary }}</p>
    <details class="explain rules"><summary>Exact rules</summary><div class="chart-instruction">{{ caller() }}</div></details>{% endmacro %}

{% macro cheap_and_good(r) %}{% call rules("Companies that are both cheap and good, ranked by the weaker of the two.") %}<ul>
  <li>The top 20 by the worse of each company's Value and Quality positions{{ r.among }}: cheap and good at once, not one making up for the other.</li>
  <li>Ties go to the better sum of the two positions.</li>
  <li>Only companies whose owner yield on the share price is above {{ r.cheap_yield }}%: what one share earns in a normal year after today's interest on its debt or cash, over the share price.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro cheapest(r) %}{% call rules("The cheapest companies for what the business earns in a normal year, among those whose moat and management hold up: many cheap companies are cheap for a reason.") %}<ul>
  <li>Down the Value ranking until 20 companies whose moat and management are both graded at least {{ r.cheapest_both }}: a company graded below is skipped and the next one taken; one not yet graded keeps its place, marked, until it is.</li>
  <li>The Value score: the normalised owner earnings of the business before interest (the cash it earns after everything it needs to stay as it is), projected 3 years on its own steady trend, declines charged, over market cap plus debt, less spare cash, so debt and cash do not change the rank.</li>
  <li>Only companies whose owner yield on the share price is above {{ r.cheap_yield }}%: the same earnings after today's interest on debt or cash, over the share price.</li>
  <li>Cyclicals at mid-cycle earnings, with no trend.</li>
  <li>Profitable operating companies only: banks, insurers, REITs and utilities are in the By kind lists, holdings in Balance sheet plays.</li>
  <li>The Quality score is not considered.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro best_businesses(r) %}{% call rules("The best businesses by returns on capital and per-share growth, among those whose moat and management are graded among the best, whatever their price.") %}<ul>
  <li>Down the Quality ranking until 20 companies whose moat and management are both graded at least {{ r.grade_best }}, one of them above it: a company graded below is skipped and the next one taken; one not yet graded keeps its place, marked, until it is.</li>
  <li>The Quality score: return on capital (ROIC; return on equity for financials) at its normal level, adjusted for its trend, shrunk when unsteady and cut for heavy debt; plus how much one share of the business grew and whether the share count shrank.</li>
  <li>Profitable operating companies only (banks, insurers, REITs and utilities are in the By kind lists, holdings in Balance sheet plays).</li>
  <li>Price is not considered: a great business can still be too dear. Cheap and good is where price and quality meet.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro compounders(r) %}{% call rules("Businesses with high returns that grow smoothly without issuing shares, at a price that already pays: companies to hold for twenty years. Two routes in, one list.") %}<ul>
  <li><strong>Steady route:</strong> normal return on capital (ROIC) of {{ r.operating_min_roic }}% or more and owner earnings of at least {{ r.operating_min_yield }}% of the price; over at least {{ r.min_years }} annual reports, revenue in US terms (the company's currency at today's rate, net of its inflation beyond the US) up every year (one flat year within 2% allowed), return on capital and operating margin never down more than {{ r.operating_max_dip }}% in a year and no lower than where they started; the share count never up; outside the industries left out below.</li>
  <li><strong>Fast route:</strong> revenue per share compounding at {{ r.fast_min_revenue }}% a year or more and owner earnings per share at {{ r.fast_min_growth }}% a year or more, in US dollars, both discounted for any bumps; owner earnings of at least {{ r.fast_min_yield }}% of the price; revenue and operating margin up every year, one flat year allowed; the share count stable or falling every year; no biotechnology.</li>
  <li>Both routes: owner earnings per share, in US terms, positive and close to their rising trend; no new shares since the last annual report; ranked companies only (no banks, insurers, holdings, REITs or utilities).</li>
  <li>Growth that earns its keep: at least {{ r.list_min_incremental }}% earned, in US terms, on the capital added since the oldest annual report.</li>
  <li>Pricing power: the gross margin now no more than {{ r.max_margin_loss }} points below where it was in the year of the highest inflation, so the costs were passed on.</li>
  <li>Little debt: net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit (EBIT) or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times;.</li>
  <li>Not cyclical, not at peak margins, past the AI screen. Through recessions: its dividend per share not cut by more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past global recession ({{ r.recessions }}, and {{ r.downturns }}; {{ r.recession_exempt }}, whose supervisors suspended dividends). A company whose dividend record reaches no recession passes, except where a recession hits hard (the industries Boring businesses names): there only an industry leader holding net cash.</li>
  <li>It keeps only the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking, in that order.</li>
</ul>
<details><summary>Industries left out of the steady route</summary><p>Biotech and speciality or generic drugs, airlines, carmakers, telecoms, solar and renewable utilities, oil and gas producers, steel, aluminium, copper, farm inputs, clothing, footwear, department stores, speciality retail, restaurants, paper, regional banks, and the industries most exposed to AI (software, IT services, consulting, staffing, advertising, publishing, education, business services, internet content, gaming, health information, entertainment, broadcasting).</p></details>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro below_book(r) %}{% call rules("Companies priced below what their balance sheet holds: tangible book value per share, net debt counted, with a book that earns its keep; ordered by the size of the discount. Operating companies and holdings.") %}<ul>
  <li>Book value and tangible book value (book less goodwill and other intangibles) per share both positive.</li>
  <li>Tangible book per share, with net debt counted against it, at or above the share price; book per share not shrinking.</li>
  <li>The book earns its keep: normal return on capital (ROIC) of {{ r.asset_min_roic }}% or more. A book is worth about what it earns, so a company earning 3% on its assets at 70% of book is no bargain.</li>
  <li>Operating companies: bounded debt, free cash flow positive in most years, Quality &ge; 35. In the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking.</li>
  <li>Holdings: their book per share with its goodwill, since leaving goodwill out would value the companies a holding bought at zero. Equity at least 6% of assets, Quality &ge; 50 (too few holdings for a best fifth of their own).</li>
  <li>Both: Value &ge; 50, positive earnings power, no dilution.</li>
  <li>Ordered by the discount: tangible book (net debt counted) as a share of the price, largest first.</li>
  <li>Not here: banks and insurers (their own lists), REITs (the Real estate list: a REIT's book is cost under US accounting, fair value under international rules, so book does not compare) and asset managers (their value is the fees they earn, which no book records).</li>
</ul><p>Net current assets (current assets less every liability; Graham's net-net test) per share are shown beside it (left blank for banks, insurers and property developers whose land sits in current assets). A net-net, priced below its net current assets, is also below its tangible book, so it enters by the same tests, return on capital included, and is marked with a <strong>net-net</strong> pill. Most net-nets lose money, and Graham bought them as a basket; this list keeps the rare one that earns.</p>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro banks(r) %}{% call rules("Banks and lenders judged against other banks and lenders only: return on equity, book and capital.") %}<ul>
  <li>Banks, credit card and consumer lenders, brokers, mortgage lenders and mortgage REITs. Not in the rankings: a lender's earnings rest on borrowed money, so its yield and returns do not compare with an operating company's.</li>
  <li>The best {{ r.top }} of banks and lenders by the worse of their Value and Quality positions among them, in that order.</li>
  <li>Value: net income one share owns against its price, among banks. Quality: return on equity restated to a standard 8% capital, its steadiness, return on assets, book growth per share and buybacks.</li>
  <li>Equity at least {{ r.equity }}% of assets; no dividend cut in the last twelve months. Its dividend through 2009 is shown, not tested (most lenders cut then; 2020 is not judged, as supervisors suspended bank dividends).</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro insurers(r) %}{% call rules("Insurers judged against other insurers only: return on equity, book and capital.") %}<ul>
  <li>Life, property and casualty, specialty insurers and reinsurers. Not in the rankings: an insurer invests its clients' premiums, so its yield and returns do not compare with an operating company's.</li>
  <li>The best {{ r.top }} of insurers by the worse of their Value and Quality positions among them, in that order.</li>
  <li>Value: net income one share owns against its price, at mid-cycle (investment gains are not separated), among insurers. Quality: return on equity, its steadiness, book growth per share and buybacks.</li>
  <li>Equity at least {{ r.equity }}% of assets; no dividend cut in the last twelve months. Its dividend through 2009 is shown, not tested.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro real_estate(r) %}{% call rules("REITs judged against other REITs only: the cash one share earns, returns, and debt they can carry.") %}<ul>
  <li>Equity REITs (property owners). Not in the rankings: their book is cost under US accounting and fair value under international rules, and they are financed long, so neither compares with an operating company's.</li>
  <li>The best {{ r.top }} of REITs by the worse of their Value and Quality positions among them, in that order. Value is the owner yield: cash after the upkeep of the buildings (capex up to depreciation), close to what REITs call AFFO.</li>
  <li>Owner yield positive; interest covered at least {{ r.reit_cover }}&times; and net debt at most {{ r.reit_net_debt }}&times; operating profit: a REIT far below book with its interest barely covered is usually a debt trap.</li>
  <li>The dividend not cut by more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past recession ({{ r.recessions }}), nor in the last twelve months: a REIT pays out most of its income, so its dividend follows the business. A record too short to reach a recession passes.</li>
  <li>Book / price is shown, not tested.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro utilities(r) %}{% call rules("Utilities judged against other utilities only: the cash one share earns, returns, and debt they can carry.") %}<ul>
  <li>Electric, gas and water utilities, power producers and renewables. Not in the rankings: their returns are regulated or contracted and their long-lived assets are financed with debt, so neither compares with an operating company's.</li>
  <li>The best {{ r.top }} of utilities by the worse of their Value and Quality positions among them, in that order. Value is the owner yield; Quality the return on capital, per-share growth and shares shrinking, each against utilities only.</li>
  <li>Owner yield positive; interest covered at least {{ r.utility_cover }}&times; and net debt at most {{ r.utility_net_debt }}&times; operating profit.</li>
  <li>The dividend not cut by more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past recession ({{ r.recessions }}), nor in the last twelve months. A record too short to reach a recession passes.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro energy(r) %}{% call rules("Energy companies with mid-cycle returns and a balance sheet that survives a price fall. They stay in the rankings; this is a clean view of them.") %}<ul>
  <li>Yahoo's Energy sector: oil and gas producers, integrated companies, pipelines, refiners, services and drilling, coal and uranium.</li>
  <li>Value &ge; {{ r.energy_value }}, Quality &ge; {{ r.energy_quality }}, normal return on capital (ROIC) &ge; {{ r.energy_roic }}%.</li>
  <li>Net cash, or net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times;, and at most {{ r.net_debt_equity }}% of equity.</li>
  <li>Not at peak margins (most of energy is cyclical and valued at mid-cycle earnings). Yahoo keeps only about four years of reports, which include the 2022 price spike, so a mid-cycle figure may still read high: the debt test is the guard.</li>
  <li>The dividend not cut by more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% through a downturn ({{ r.recessions }}, and the oil price collapse of {{ r.downturns }}), nor in the last twelve months. A company that pays no dividend has no record and passes.</li>
  <li>In combined rank order, not cut to the best {{ r.top }} (too few would pass).</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}


{% macro income(r) %}{% call rules("Dividends a foreign investor keeps (statutory withholding at most " ~ r.income_wht ~ "%), paid every year for at least a decade, never more than " ~ r.income_dip ~ "% below the year before, ordered by what they pay now plus how fast they can grow.") %}<ul>
  <li>Withholding tax on dividends for a foreign individual of {{ r.income_wht }}% or less, at the statutory rate before any treaty (one yardstick for every reader).</li>
  <li>The regular dividend per share paid in each of at least {{ r.income_years }} complete years, no year more than {{ r.income_dip }}% below the year before, nor the last twelve months below the twelve before by as much (with as many payments in each), and the dividend no lower than ten years ago, in US terms. Most companies at low withholding (China, Thailand, Malaysia, the Gulf) pay a fixed share of their earnings, so their dividend moves with them; a small dip in a soft year is that policy, not trouble.</li>
  <li>Judged payment by payment, in the currency the dividend is declared in. A special dividend (a payment at least {{ r.special_ratio }}&times; its season's usual size, higher than the same payment a year before and a year after) is left out, so the year after it is not a dip. A payment that slipped across New Year, or moved earlier when an interim dividend was added, is no dip either.</li>
  <li>Yield after withholding of {{ r.income_yield }}% or more; the dividend at most {{ r.income_payout }}% of what one share earns after interest at zero growth, so it is paid from today's earnings.</li>
  <li>Normal return on capital (on equity for banks and insurers) of {{ r.income_roic }}% or more.</li>
  <li>Lasting: not cyclical, not at peak margins, past the AI screen, the dividend not cut more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past recession ({{ r.recessions }}, and {{ r.downturns }}).</li>
  <li>Little debt: net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times;, and at most {{ r.net_debt_equity }}% of equity (banks and insurers: equity at least {{ r.equity }}% of assets).</li>
  <li>It keeps only the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking; a bank or an insurer, ranked apart, the best {{ r.top }} of its own kind.</li>
  <li><strong>Order</strong>: the income return, yield after withholding plus the dividend's growth, the dividend's own expected return. The growth counted is the regular dividend's 10-year growth in US terms, but no faster than the business can fund from the earnings it keeps (return on capital &times; the share not paid out), and at most {{ r.income_growth_cap }}% a year: past that a company is a growth stock with a dividend.</li>
  <li><strong>Spread over loan</strong> (shown, not tested): the yield after withholding less what a euro margin loan costs today, {{ r.borrow_rate }}% (the ECB's euro short-term rate plus 1.5% of broker margin and 0.5% of safety). Borrowing to buy adds risk: the loan, its interest and the price stay yours.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro low_beta(r) %}{% call rules("Steady businesses whose share has moved little with the market, at a price that already pays: for a holder who wants to sleep well, or who borrows against the holding.") %}<ul>
  <li>Beta below {{ r.beta_max }}: Yahoo's, from 5 years of monthly returns against the S&amp;P 500. The only list a share's price path tests. For a share outside the US it partly says how little it moves with Wall Street, not how little it can fall, so the list leans to Asia and the Gulf.</li>
  <li>Owner yield on the share price above {{ r.cheap_yield }}%: what one share earns in a normal year after today's interest on its debt or cash, over the share price. Ordered by it, highest first.</li>
  <li>Steady figures in every year on file: revenue never down more than {{ r.beta_revenue }}% in a year, free cash flow positive every year, owner earnings not trending down, gross margin within {{ r.beta_gm_sd }} points (standard deviation) and return on capital steady (spread around its trend at most {{ r.beta_roic_var }}&times;). A missing figure fails.</li>
  <li>Lasting: not cyclical, not at peak margins, past the AI screen; a dividend, when paid, not cut more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past recession. A company paying none is judged on its figures.</li>
  <li>Little debt: net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times;, and at most {{ r.net_debt_equity }}% of equity.</li>
  <li>Operating companies only, from the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro shortlist(r) %}{% call rules("Boring businesses worth researching in depth: dull, essential businesses in industries that do not attract new capital and that AI cannot easily replace; cheap, good, cleanly reported, and through stress tests for recession and debt.") %}<p>At most {{ r.n }} companies passing every test the figures allow, at once. A test whose figure is missing is passed.</p><ul>
  <li><strong>Ranked</strong>: profitable operating companies in the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking.</li>
  <li><strong>Growth without dilution</strong>: revenue per share not falling, shares not issued, owner earnings per share not shrinking, at least {{ r.incremental }}% earned on the capital added.</li>
  <li><strong>Cash</strong>: free cash flow positive in at least {{ r.fcf }}% of the years.</li>
  <li><strong>Clean accounts</strong>: Beneish M-score (a test for manipulated earnings) at most {{ r.beneish }}; accruals (profit not backed by operating cash) at most {{ r.accruals }}% of assets.</li>
  <li><strong>Debt</strong>: net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit (EBIT) or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times;; net debt at most {{ r.net_debt_equity }}% of equity, so higher rates and inflation can be carried; the gross margin now no more than {{ r.max_margin_loss }} points below where it was in the year of the highest inflation, so costs can be passed on.</li>
  <li><strong>Holding up</strong>: analysts' estimates not cut by more than {{ r.estimate_cut }}% in 90 days; recent revenue and operating profit not down more than {{ r.revenue_fall }}% and {{ r.ebit_fall }}%; no dividend cut in the last twelve months.</li>
  <li><strong>Stress tests</strong>: no industry naturally fragile or exposed to AI (software, IT services, consulting, staffing, advertising, publishing, education, business services, internet content, gaming, health information, entertainment, broadcasting); where a global recession hits hard, and for any cyclical, only the industry's leaders (top {{ r.leader_share }}% by revenue among the benchmark's companies, the top {{ r.leader_min }} always) holding net cash.</li>
  <li><strong>A boring industry</strong>: one that does not attract new capital, so the incumbents face few new competitors: {{ r.boring_industries }}.</li>
  <li><strong>Price</strong>: earnings power at zero growth (and the owner yield, if lower) of at least {{ r.hurdle }}% of the price, the higher of 6% and the 10-year Treasury (a business should pay more than a bond before any growth; growth is the margin of safety), plus how much more than the US the company's home government pays to borrow dollars (its rating-based default spread, from Damodaran's January table, less the US's, in points: {{ r.spreads }}; the country of its headquarters, not of its sales); Value &ge; {{ r.value }}.</li>
  <li><strong>Quality</strong>: normalised return on capital of {{ r.roic }}% or more, Quality &ge; {{ r.quality }}.</li>
  <li><strong>Grade</strong>: a stricter moat and management bar than the other lists': both grades at least {{ r.grade_both }} <em>and</em> one of them {{ r.grade_top }} or more (the other lists: both at least {{ r.grade_both }}, or either at least {{ r.grade_one }}).</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro trouble(r) %}{% call rules("Boring businesses in temporary trouble: every test of Boring businesses but one, nothing breaking now, which they fail. Sales, profit or analysts' estimates are falling right now, and the price shows it; the bet is that the trouble passes and the business stays.") %}<ul>
  <li>Every other test of Boring businesses, as there: a boring industry past the AI screen, growing per share without dilution, cash-backed and clean earnings, a sound balance sheet, recession leaders only, the margin kept through inflation, owner earnings at zero growth (EPV) beating the bond, durable returns, the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking.</li>
  <li>Nothing breaking now, failed: recent revenue down more than {{ r.revenue_fall }}% or operating profit (EBIT) down more than {{ r.ebit_fall }}% (the latest quarters against the year before), earnings estimates cut more than {{ r.estimate_cut }}% in 90 days, or a dividend cut paid in the last 12 months.</li>
  <li>Graded before it counts: moat and management both at least {{ r.trouble_both }} and either at least {{ r.trouble_top }}; an ungraded company is folded away until graded.</li>
  <li>In combined-rank order. Read why the numbers fall before buying: a temporary trouble and a broken business look alike at first.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

